decisions/gates.md:432:## GATES-19 · The landing queue: behind-main is a judged merge, readiness order with an age bound, verdicts carried only under a byte-identical input key (2026-10-19/20, ROOT rulings; design docs/record/GATES-19-DESIGN.md)
decisions/gates.md:457:- queue (land.py on a scratch ref, tickets from gate-lib.sh new_ticket): a lander still judging blocks no ready one (`queue: head (displaced 0 times)`); a twice-displaced live ticket makes a ready younger lander print `queue: waiting for 00000003.2204417 (displaced 2): displaced 2 times, it is head regardless (GATES-19)`, the ref does not move until the old one lands, and the younger is then displaced (75, count 1); a dead head (holder stopped by its PID) is skipped and the waiter lands; the age bound: displaced on passes 1 and 2, then `A pass 3 exit=0` while a younger ready D waits and is displaced after; a stale base is still refused by the CAS.
decisions/gates.md:465:**Measured first day (GATES-8, 2026-10-19, every scripts/commit log on hbox under the new queue).** 13 landers logged `queue: head (displaced N)`: N=0: 5, N=1: 1, N=2: 6, N=3: 1 (HOST-61, bd39f78). Reaching the bound is the common case (6 of 13). Every pass beyond 3 was a TIE, which the age bound allows: two landers twice-displaced at once (tickets 21, 22 at c7f455e), then four (28, 33, 35, 36 at bd39f78 → 224e9fe; OPS-18 oldest). k tied landers landing serially onto each other's tips cost the youngest k−1 more re-bases by construction (each is judged on the tip it lands onto), so no ordering rule bounds that below 2 + k; ROOT withdrew a 4-pass rule on this. What shortens a tie re-base is reuse (GATES-20); a merge-train is GATES-21, PROPOSED (docs/record/GATES-21-PROPOSED.md), built only if the measured tail stays long under GATES-20. Admission control (refusing entry while ties stand) is refused: it shortens nothing a lander can see and starves the newest.
decisions/gates.md:467:## GATES-20a · A run is selected by every binary it runs, not only its own: spawning rows, declared drives, and names in the static key (2026-10-20, ROOT ruling; found by GATES-8's traced read-sets)
decisions/gates.md:472:- GATES-19's critique (e) said no exe walks the tree; `cargo:devtools:workspace` does: it stats every entry under the root, decisions/ included (`crates()`), so a `decisions/x/Cargo.toml` changes its verdict while GATES-19's static key held no decisions/ path at all.
decisions/gates.md:484:**Critique.** (a) Spawning rows select on every exe's closure: rows and store-plants run on nearly every Lean landing. They are seconds, and the traced read-set (GATES-20) is what narrows them to what they actually run. (b) The lean_bin scan reads string literals; an exe name built at run time (`format!`) escapes it, and only a trace sees that: the standing trace should be a check, not a measurement (GATES-20). (c) The tests/common over-approximation names drives a test never runs.
decisions/gates.md:485:**Meta-critique.** GATES-19 keyed reuse on "every binary" precisely because a run may drive any other, and never asked the same question of selection: the key and the selection answered "what can move this run" differently, and only the key was right. The invariant now stated: a row is selected whenever a binary it may run can change.
decisions/gates.md:487:## GATES-20b · The landed copy: every path is merged from what the lander recorded, never taken from the working tree; a landing that would revert a recent commit is refused `stale path` unless named; a head lander is never charged for a tie (2026-10-21, ROOT rulings; HOSTING-13's hazard, LANG-21's finding)
decisions/gates.md:489:**Why.** HOSTING-13 caught by hand, mid-judge, that a re-based attempt re-read `launcher/tests/members.rs` from the working tree while CLIENT-31 (cdc0e9a) had added 70 lines to it on main. Verified at source (hbox logs/gates9-verify.sh; a scratch worktree, main's own judge with a stub pre-push that prints the candidate and refuses, so nothing could land): GATES-19's merge ran only for a path whose copy at the worktree's HEAD differed from main's, with HEAD's copy as the base, and the candidate was then `git add -A` of the working copy. HEAD stands in for "the copy the lander edited", and nothing checks that. **V1**: a copy made from a1ca640 plus one lane line, written into a worktree at main (a scratch copy copied in, the scp workflow several lanes use): candidate `members.rs | 81 +++----`, 1003 lines, CLIENT-31's lines gone and the lane line in. **V2**: worktree at a1ca640, lane line added, then `git reset main` (HEAD moved, the file left): the same 1003-line candidate. **V0** (control): HEAD left at a1ca640: merged, 1077 lines. A green judge lands V1 and V2. HOSTING-39 itself (ea52a4a) was safe: its HEAD stayed at a1ca640, so its attempt 5 merged. How GATES-19 missed it: its plants put the lane's HEAD at the lane's real base each time, the one case where HEAD is right. The rewrite case was also open: the working file was re-read on every attempt, so a file rewritten between attempts (P4 below) landed whole.
decisions/gates.md:492:1. **The landed copy comes from a record, never from the working tree** (gates/rebase.py `snapshot`/`candidate`). The lander's first attempt records, per file under its PATH..., BASE = the blob at the worktree's HEAD then and OURS = the working copy then (written to the object store), in `<lander dir>/snapshot.tsv`. Every attempt builds its candidate from that record against main's blob M: OURS = M nothing; OURS = BASE (not edited) main's copy; BASE = M (main did not change it) OURS; otherwise `git merge-file OURS BASE M`, a conflict refused by name with its hunks, an absent side refused. The working tree is not read again during the landing. After the compare-and-swap `writeback` writes the landed copy into each working file that still holds OURS. A file edited while the lander ran keeps that edit, merged 3-way onto the landed copy (base = OURS), as an uncommitted edit, or is named when it does not merge. GATES-19's merged.tsv is deleted, because the record makes it unnecessary.
decisions/gates.md:509:**What the old judge says about this commit:** main's scripts/commit and gates/rebase.py build its candidate (the HEAD-based merge) and main's pre-push judges it; its paths are GATES' own. **What the new judge would have said:** the same candidate. wt/gates was detached at main before launch (ROOT's mitigation), so its record equals main plus these four files, and `stale` finds no revert in them (rebase.py's old `merge` is deleted, the block GATES-19 added: that is 6527d9f's/1a6163b's hunk being replaced, which GATES names for itself).
decisions/gates.md:512:**Meta-critique.** GATES-19 trusted HEAD as "the copy the lander edited" and its plants never moved HEAD. The defect lived in what the plants held fixed. This decision's own blind spot is the window; the measure that would expose it is the age of the oldest copy a lane copies in, which nothing records.
decisions/gates.md:514:## GATES-20 · Traced read-sets select the runs and key the reuse; the judge's code is main's, the candidate supplies only its table (2026-10-20, landed 10-21 after GATES-20b; ROOT rulings F1/F2 and "the judge's CODE is main's"; design docs/record/GATES-20-DESIGN.md)
decisions/gates.md:520:  1. its reach (GATES-20a) meets the changed .lean modules;
decisions/gates.md:527:- **The judge's code is main's.** pre-push runs `$J/runs.py` and `$J/waste.py` (J = the judging scripts' tree: main's copy under scripts/commit). The candidate supplies only its runs.tsv and waste.tsv; a candidate's gates/*.py is never executed by its own judge. Consequence (ROOT): a new row KIND and the first rows that use it are two commits, runner first. Why: GATES-19 (1a6163b) and GATES-20a (6527d9f) both changed gates/runs.py and were judged by their own runner. Standing on each read GREEN (1a6163beb767, 6527d9f146e0), and that was the only check after the fact.
decisions/gates.md:529:- **Reads outside the tree (F2) are reported, not RED.** runs.py prints `outside EXE: home N, tmp M`, and standing's line carries `outside=N(traced|untraced)`. **GATES-20c** turns it RED. Trigger: the census below is sorted. Dated: 2026-10-27.
