typed; the proposal's dependent-context experiment is modelled by a guard parametrized by `n`, not by `Vec n`. (5) The
rows demonstrate one hole per object; many holes per object (the `waits` row design) and the `Std` generators are not
built. (6) 1,407 lines in one proof module (`Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean`), a law-8 split owed as a follow-up `lang:`. (7) Named limits from the instance lane: fillings are typed in the empty meta-context (no hole inside a filling, so hierarchical refinement is not expressible); hole-name uniqueness lives in `Template`, not in `HasTy` (the instance takes the first declaration of a name); fillings compare syntactically, so β-equal fillings are different candidates.
**Meta-critique.** The first design mapped the proposal onto nano's existing vocabulary and called the obend package the
deliverable; an adversarial read found 8 HIGH defects in that mapping and Ember named the substitution for what it was.
The pull was familiarity: pins and rows were already proved, so I reached for them instead of the theory. The second pull,
in this landing, is to call the abstractly-instantiated closure "the CMTT closure" — it is not until `cmttCalculus`
lands, which is why (a) is named above and the commit message says so.

## LANG-68 Lang's deployed term language is a lawful instance of the guarded contextual closure, on its closed-hole fragment (2026-10-20 real; ACTIVITIES-2; ROOT ruled fork (A); cv 01a158c0-ddbf step 3's foundation; the (B) fork is Ember's card, cv 01a158da-0192)
**Why.** LANG-66's closure theory was instantiated by its toy calculus and by CMTT (`cmttLaws`); the generators that make the
Bell an instance (LANG-66 (d)) need the closure's carrier to be what a nano package is written in. ACTIVITIES-1's handoff
asked whether that instance can be HONEST: what are `uses`/`drop`/`strengthen`/`weaken` for a Lang filling such that
`weaken_avoids` and `uses_var` discharge non-degenerately (READER-CONTEXTUAL's HIGH: `drop := id` or `uses ≡ false`
satisfy the strengthening laws)? Answered by building it, not by prose.
**Decision.** `Lang/Contextual/LangCalculus.lean` (def module, 568 lines, no theorem) + `Lang/Proofs/Contextual/LangInstance.lean`
(proof module, 46 theorems): `langData : CalculusData` with `Tm := Lang.Term` (the DEPLOYED term, `Lang/Term.lean`), not
`Lang.Data`: a data value has no variables, so `uses_var`/`hasTy_var` could be met only by bolting a variable onto data,
the lookalike the reader refused. `check Ψ M A := decide (infer [] Ψ M = some A)` (the deployed checker, sound and complete
by `infer_iff`); `uses`/`weaken`/`strengthen` a binder-aware de Bruijn rename (`Term.renameVar`, `lam` and every `case` arm
bind one; `liftIdx`/`lowerIdx` from Core); `drop := eraseIdx`; `var := Term.var`; `lookup := Ψ[x]?`; a hole is `ref n`
(the only hole shape a `Term` has) and `msubst := Term.fillRefs` (closed fillings, no shifting); a structural
`Term.decEq` by hand (17 constructors, nested rows; `Term` had no decidable equality). `LangHasTy Ω Γ t T :=
(∀ d ∈ Ω, d.ctx = []) ∧ RefsDeclared Ω t ∧ HasType (ΔOf Ω) Γ t T` with `ΔOf Ω` name-indexed (hole `n` at package position
`n`, LANG-66's `holeEnv` move) and `RefsDeclared` = every `ref` the template uses is a declared hole (it is what refutes
`msubst := id` and what makes `ΔOf`'s `.nat` placeholder at an undeclared name make no theorem true: `usesRef_fillRefs`
+ `refsDeclared_nil`). `theorem langLaws : CalculusLaws langData LangHasTy`: msubst_lemma ← `fillRefs_typed`
(+ `tyOf_filter`, `ΔOf_getElem?`, `hasType_closed_lift`: a closed filling types under every Γ, used exactly at the `lam`/arm
binders where capture would otherwise occur); check_sound/complete ← `Lang.infer_sound/complete` + `langHasTy_nil_iff`
(whose right-to-left needs `hasType_usesRef`: a term typed under Δ = [] uses no `ref`); strengthen_lemma ←
`hasType_unshiftAt`; strengthen_factor ← `Term.shiftAt_unshiftAt`; weaken_avoids ← `usesVar_shiftAt` (unconditional);
hasTy_var ← `HasType.var`; uses_var by `simp`. Toys: `lang_strengthen_toy` (`strengthen 1 (app (var 0) (var 2)) =
app (var 0) (var 1)`, weakened back: the strengthening is not the identity); `lang_template_toy` (`app (ref 0) (nat 3)`
typed WITH the hole `⟨0, [], nat → nat⟩`, NOT without it — refuted by `RefsDeclared`, and by `HasType` too since
`ΔOf [] = []` — filled with `λx.x` by `rfl`, typed with the hole gone through `langLaws.msubst_lemma`).
**The boundary, as a theorem.** `langTemplate_no_hyps : Template.Typed tpl LangHasTy → ∀ I ∈ tpl.Ω, I.hyps = []`. A Lang
`ref` is a closed package reference: it carries no local context and no substitution, so every hole of a typed Lang
template is CLOSED, and three closure paths are EMPTY ON EVERY LANG TEMPLATE: the `strengthening` outcome
(`repairSelected`'s `D.uses` branch), the alternative-by-hypothesis outcome, and hypothesis-derived supports
(`effSupports`' second summand). They are exercised only by the CMTT instance. So the bridge the generators build
(LANG-66 (d), next) is over the FULL `CalculusLaws` restricted to the closed-hole fragment, not over a sub-structure of
the laws; the fragment's name is this theorem.
**Price.** No landed theorem gains a premise; no landed statement changes (READER-CONTEXTUAL: `git diff --stat HEAD`
empty before the two new files; nothing landed imports them). SIZE: two modules, one proof module, ~1,380 Lang lines (568
deployed), 46 theorems, max dependents +1 (the instance is OVER the deployed checker: one more dependent of its closure).
**Vacuity read.** READER-CONTEXTUAL (`a883dc3fb9dca28c8`, resumed), 47 theorems with the disjunct check per theorem,
each iff's two sides and each ∃ witness named: LAND-WITH-FIXES, nothing vacuous or false; the two LOWs applied
(`langHasTy_closed_holes` was `h.1`, a projection: DELETED, the conjunct is cited instead; `ΔOf`'s docstring names the
theorems that make its placeholder harmless); the MEDIUM is a closure-design item made visible by this instance, filed
cv 01a158da-017c for CLOSURE: no operation requires `Template.Typed` (C3 moved it from the structure to a predicate), so a
closure over an UNTYPED template with a non-empty hole ctx reports `verified` for an open filling (`check [nat] (var 0)
nat`) that `fillRefs` would place unshifted — no landed theorem claims that state Derivable (every derivability theorem
takes `ht`), but the report is the checker's verdict in a context the template never justified; its own `lang:` with its
own read; the generators state Typed for every generated iface (ctx = [] by construction).
**Alternatives.** (i) `Tm := Lang.Data` with a variable constructor bolted on: refused, the reader's degenerate class in
new clothes. (ii) A curried encoding of contextual holes (`ref n : fnOf Ψ A`, occurrences `app (ref n) σ…`): refused
here because `msubst` must then wrap fillings in `lam`s over Ψ, which needs Ω — a `CalculusData` signature change
(`msubst` taking Ω) restating `msubst_lemma`/`Derivable` and everything through them; not foreclosed. (iii) (B) CMTT's
`u[σ]` as an 18th `Term` constructor `hole n σ`: the honest contextual instance in the DEPLOYED language; a `lang!`
(checker, BigStep, printer/reader, the `lang/package` wire with CODEC review, every Term induction one case each, every
package re-ids; premises added: none known). ROOT ruled (A) now and sent (B) to Ember as a design fork: under (A) the
strengthening outcome is a theorem of the MODEL only, reachable by no deployed package — the honestly-labelled-caveat
class; the question is whether nano's deployed language should carry what its theory proves. (A) does not foreclose (B):
the generators' statements are over `LangHasTy` and survive it losing the `d.ctx = []` conjunct.
**Critique.** (1) The instance proves the laws for ALL Lang terms, but every template the laws reach has closed holes;
the contextual half of "contextual modal types" is, for Lang, a theorem about a fragment nothing deployed inhabits
(the card above). (2) `fillRefs` does not shift: sound only because fillings are closed, which `LangHasTy []` forces;
a future (B) must add the shift. (3) `ΔOf` is positional over a name-indexed list with a placeholder; the honest shape
would index `HasType`'s Δ by name, which is a change to the deployed checker's signature, refused for this landing.
(4) `Term.decEq` is 272 hand-listed off-diagonal cases (the Ty.lean idiom); `deriving` is refused by Lean v4.30.0 on
the nested inductive. (5) The reader's MEDIUM is older than this landing (C3's restructure of LANG-66 made `Template.Typed`
a predicate no operation demands); this instance is the first with a non-trivial ctx to make it bite.
**Meta-critique.** The handoff's question had a tempting prose answer ("the bridge is over a data-only sub-structure of
the laws"); it was WRONG in both directions — the laws discharge in full, and the restriction is on the templates, not
the laws — and only building the instance showed where the restriction actually sits. The pull to resist next is to let
`langTemplate_no_hyps` read as a limit of Lang rather than as a design choice ROOT made and Ember may reverse.

## LANG-69 Split the contextual closure proof module by responsibility (2026-10-20 real; CX-ACT; cv 01a158c0-ddae)
**Decision.** Replace the 1,533-line Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean with eight proof modules:
ClosureStatements (calculus laws and judgments), Judge (tokens, judgment, provenance, withdrawal, lookup),
Repair (candidate pass, coherence, locality and the exact work log), Outcomes (soundness of repair outcomes),
Operations (operation frames and history prefix), Soundness (conditional soundness and preserved invariants),
History (selection, acceptance, consumer boundary and sessions), Stability (value guards and grounding).
CoreInstance and LangInstance import ClosureStatements only; Experiments imports History and Stability.
There is no compatibility import hub. This is moves plus imports and module wrappers, with NO statement changes.
**Price premises.** None. No landed theorem gains a hypothesis; none is restated. No definition in the deployed
language changes, no package re-ids, no wire moves. LANG-68's LangCalculus line count was 567, not its recorded 568.
**Verification.** A source partition audit accounts for every original line 32..1532 exactly once, preserving
all 102 declaration names (89 theorems) and every statement/proof body byte-for-byte. The scoped build of
Experiments, CoreInstance and LangInstance is GREEN (59 jobs), under 6G/two threads and Lean's finite default
heartbeat cap. The original statements and their disjunct readings are the LANG-66 reader's unchanged statements;
this change adds no assertion, disjunct or premise. No new semantic check is added, so no new mutation plant
is applicable to these moves. The consumer build checks resolution and composition after changing imports.
**Alternatives.** Keep an import-only Closure compatibility module: rejected because it preserves a broad
dependency entry point rather than teaching each consumer which family it needs. Split by line count: rejected
because it makes the module boundaries arbitrary and leaves unrelated dependencies together.
**Critique.** Soundness still joins operation frames with repair outcome lemmas, and History consequently imports
both; this follows the current proof dependencies, not an ideal independent theory. A further split should be
driven by an actual consumer or rebuild bottleneck, not a target module count.
**Meta-critique.** Moving code can masquerade as simplification. Here the measurable benefit is that the two
calculus instances no longer depend on the 89 closure theorems; only their laws/judgments are imported. The
statement/body equality audit prevents a proof cleanup from slipping into this structural change.
/-
# Lang.Contextual.Closure — the guarded contextual closure `(P, Ω, σ, D, support, Φ, t)`

The object of ACTIVITIES-PROPOSAL doc 2 §9, over an ABSTRACT calculus (its data `CalculusData` here, its laws
`CalculusLaws` in the proof module), so the theory
does not wait on a particular CMTT core. Two instances exist: the toy of
`Lang.Proofs.Contextual.Experiments`, and the CMTT core's, `Lang/Proofs/Contextual/CoreInstance.lean`,
which lands as the second commit.

Limits, stated: fillings are typed in the EMPTY meta-context (`HasTy [] Ψ M A`: no hole inside a
hole's filling); supports are one level (tokens, never another hole's selection), so a support cycle
is not expressible here; an environment edit touches every candidate (dependency sets are
sufficient, not minimal).

* `P, Ω` — the retained `Template`: a term typed under an open interface of holes `u :: [Ψ ⊢ A]`,
  each with its guard, budget, hypothesis tokens and POLARITY (`chooser`, `acceptor`). Kept apart
  from every environment: resubstitution needs it.
* `σ` — `Closure.sigma`: the current partial meta-substitution, the selected filling per hole.
* `D` — the selected candidate per hole (`Slot.selected`): WHICH derivation stands behind `σ`. Two
  candidates with one filling and different supports are one `σ` and two `D`s (provenance `ab + c`).
* `support` — `Closure.support`: the tokens the selected candidates rely on (`effSupports`: the
  declared supports plus the hypothesis tokens of every context variable the filling USES).
* `Φ` — `Closure.Φ`: residual obligations (derived from the statuses) plus `owed` (established by
  the accepted history and never derived away).
* `t` — `Closure.hist`: the accepted interaction history, append-only.

A candidate's status is computed by ONE function, `judge`, from the hole's checker and guard, the
world's environment and the liveness of the candidate's dependencies; the status is never the
proposer's opinion. Assumption tokens are immutable and versioned (`Token`); a world says which are
live. Withdrawal is a liveness change: it can suspend, it never refutes (`conflict` is reachable
only from the checker and the guard, which read no token).
-/

namespace Nanodregg.Lang.Contextual

/-! ## The abstract calculus -/

/-- A hole declaration `u :: [Ψ ⊢ A]`: the hole's name, its local context, its type. -/
structure HoleDecl (Ctx Ty : Type) where
  name : Nat
  ctx : Ctx
  ty : Ty

/-- The DATA of a contextual calculus the closure theory uses (its laws, with the typing relation,
are `Lang.Proofs.Contextual.Closure.CalculusLaws`). -/
structure CalculusData where
  Ty : Type
  Ctx : Type
  Tm : Type
  tmEq : DecidableEq Tm
  /-- Simultaneous meta-substitution `⟦σ⟧P` for a PARTIAL `σ` (unfilled holes stay). -/
  msubst : (Nat → Option Tm) → Tm → Tm
  /-- The checker: a proposed filling acquires authority only through it. -/
  check : Ctx → Tm → Ty → Bool
  /-- `uses M x`: the filling mentions hypothesis `x` of its context (free-variable occurrence). -/
  uses : Tm → Nat → Bool
  /-- The context without hypothesis `x`. -/
  drop : Ctx → Nat → Ctx
  /-- Strengthening `t ≡ t₀[π]`: `strengthen x t` is `t₀`, `weaken x` is `π`. -/
  strengthen : Nat → Tm → Tm
  weaken : Nat → Tm → Tm
  /-- The variable term for hypothesis `x`. -/
  var : Nat → Tm
  /-- The type a context gives hypothesis `x`, if it has one. -/
  lookup : Ctx → Nat → Option Ty

instance (D : CalculusData) : DecidableEq D.Tm := D.tmEq

/-! ## Assumption tokens and worlds -/

/-- An immutable, versioned assumption. Re-using an id at a new version is a NEW token: the
proposition a token stands for never changes. -/
structure Token where
  id : Nat
  ver : Nat
deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A world: which tokens are live, and the guard environment (dependent-context parameters). -/
structure World (E : Type) where
  live : Token → Bool
  env : E

variable {E : Type}

def World.set (w : World E) (t : Token) (b : Bool) : World E :=
  { w with live := fun t' => if t' = t then b else w.live t' }

/-- A primitive change of world. -/
inductive Edit (E : Type) where
  | withdraw (t : Token)
  | assert (t : Token)
  | setEnv (e : E)

def Edit.apply : Edit E → World E → World E
  | .withdraw t, w => w.set t false
  | .assert t, w => w.set t true
/- Guarded contextual closure: ClosureStatements. Moved without statement changes from Closure. -/
import Lang.Contextual.Closure

namespace Nanodregg.Lang.Contextual

variable {D : CalculusData} {E : Type}
  {HasTy : List (HoleDecl D.Ctx D.Ty) → D.Ctx → D.Tm → D.Ty → Prop}


/-! ## The laws of a calculus, and the statements over closures -/

/-- The LAWS of a contextual calculus over its data `D` and a typing relation `HasTy`
(`HasTy Ω Ψ M A` is `Ω; Ψ ⊢ M : A`, meta-context `Ω`), each named after the CMTT fact it stands for. -/
structure CalculusLaws (D : CalculusData)
    (HasTy : List (HoleDecl D.Ctx D.Ty) → D.Ctx → D.Tm → D.Ty → Prop) : Prop where
  /-- CMTT's meta-substitution lemma, partial form: the filled holes leave the meta-context. -/
  msubst_lemma : ∀ {Ω : List (HoleDecl D.Ctx D.Ty)} {Γ : D.Ctx} {P : D.Tm} {T : D.Ty}
    (σ : Nat → Option D.Tm), HasTy Ω Γ P T →
    (∀ d ∈ Ω, ∀ M, σ d.name = some M → HasTy [] d.ctx M d.ty) →
    HasTy (Ω.filter fun d => (σ d.name).isNone) Γ (D.msubst σ P) T
  check_sound : ∀ {Ψ : D.Ctx} {M : D.Tm} {A : D.Ty}, D.check Ψ M A = true → HasTy [] Ψ M A
  /-- The checker is complete: a `false` verdict is a refutation of typing, not an omission. -/
  check_complete : ∀ {Ψ : D.Ctx} {M : D.Tm} {A : D.Ty}, HasTy [] Ψ M A → D.check Ψ M A = true
  strengthen_lemma : ∀ {Ψ : D.Ctx} {M : D.Tm} {A : D.Ty} {x : Nat}, HasTy [] Ψ M A →
    D.uses M x = false → HasTy [] (D.drop Ψ x) (D.strengthen x M) A
  strengthen_factor : ∀ {M : D.Tm} {x : Nat}, D.uses M x = false → D.weaken x (D.strengthen x M) = M
  /-- Non-degeneracy of `drop`/`weaken`: a term typed WITHOUT `x`, weakened back, does not use `x`.
  With `hasTy_var`/`uses_var` it refutes both degenerate choices: `uses ≡ false` (refuted by
  `uses_var`) and `drop Ψ _ = Ψ` with `weaken = id` (`drop_removes`). -/
  weaken_avoids : ∀ {Ψ : D.Ctx} {M : D.Tm} {A : D.Ty} {x : Nat}, HasTy [] (D.drop Ψ x) M A →
    D.uses (D.weaken x M) x = false
  /-- The variable rule. -/
  hasTy_var : ∀ {Ψ : D.Ctx} {x : Nat} {A : D.Ty}, D.lookup Ψ x = some A → HasTy [] Ψ (D.var x) A
  /-- A variable uses itself. -/
  uses_var : ∀ {x : Nat}, D.uses (D.var x) x = true

/-- The template types at its closed type under the open interface of its holes. -/
def Template.Typed (tpl : Template D E)
    (HasTy : List (HoleDecl D.Ctx D.Ty) → D.Ctx → D.Tm → D.Ty → Prop) : Prop :=
  HasTy (tpl.Ω.map Iface.decl) tpl.Γ tpl.P tpl.T

/-- A monomial holds in a world: its positive literals live, its negative literals dead. -/
def Monomial.holds (w : World E) (m : List Token × List Token) : Prop :=
  (∀ t ∈ m.1, w.live t = true) ∧ (∀ b ∈ m.2, w.live b = false)

/-- DERIVABILITY: the template with the selected fillings types at its closed type, under the
meta-context of the holes still unfilled. -/
def Derivable (HasTy : List (HoleDecl D.Ctx D.Ty) → D.Ctx → D.Tm → D.Ty → Prop) (cl : Closure D E) :
    Prop :=
  HasTy ((cl.tpl.Ω.map Iface.decl).filter fun d => (cl.sigma d.name).isNone)
    cl.tpl.Γ (D.msubst cl.sigma cl.tpl.P) cl.tpl.T

/-- A hole's selection names a retained candidate whose filling passes the hole's checker. -/
def SlotFilled (I : Iface D E) (s : Slot D) : Prop :=
  ∀ i, s.selected = some i → ∃ c, s.cands[i]? = some c ∧ D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty = true

/-- Every selection is well filled. -/
def WellFilled (cl : Closure D E) : Prop :=
  ∀ I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω, SlotFilled I (cl.slot I.name)

/-- Every selected candidate's RECORDED status is `verified` (nothing awaits repair). -/
def Settled (cl : Closure D E) : Prop :=
  ∀ I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω, ∀ i, (cl.slot I.name).selected = some i →
    ∃ c, (cl.slot I.name).cands[i]? = some c ∧ c.status = .verified

/-- AVAILABILITY: every support of every selected candidate is live in `w`. -/
def Available (w : World E) (cl : Closure D E) : Prop :=
  ∀ I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω, ∀ c, cl.selectedCand I.name = some c → ∀ t ∈ effSupports I c, w.live t = true

/-- The recorded statuses are the judgment at `w`. -/
def Coherent (w : World E) (cl : Closure D E) : Prop :=
  ∀ I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω, ∀ c ∈ (cl.slot I.name).cands, c.status = judge I w c

/-- The guard is a function of the filling's VALUE only. -/
def ValueGuard {V : Type} (value : D.Tm → V) (g : E → D.Tm → Bool) : Prop :=
  ∃ g' : E → V → Bool, ∀ e t, g e t = g' e (value t)

/-- The guard's verdict is stable under the term map `s`. -/
def StableUnder (g : E → D.Tm → Bool) (s : D.Tm → D.Tm) : Prop :=
  ∀ e t, g e (s t) = g e t

/-- Least-fixpoint grounding: an atom holds iff a well-founded derivation from live facts exists. -/
inductive Derives (R : List Rule) (F : Nat → Bool) : Nat → Prop where
  | fact {x : Nat} : F x = true → Derives R F x
  | rule {r : Rule} : r ∈ R → (∀ y ∈ r.body, Derives R F y) → Derives R F r.head



end Nanodregg.Lang.Contextual
/- Guarded contextual closure: Repair. Moved without statement changes from Closure. -/
import Lang.Proofs.Contextual.Judge

namespace Nanodregg.Lang.Contextual

variable {D : CalculusData} {E : Type}
  {HasTy : List (HoleDecl D.Ctx D.Ty) → D.Ctx → D.Tm → D.Ty → Prop}


/-! ## Repair, candidate by candidate -/

theorem judge_restatus {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {c : Candidate D} {st : Status} :
    judge I w { c with status := st } = judge I w c := rfl

theorem restatus_self (c : Candidate D) : { c with status := c.status } = c := by cases c; rfl

theorem repairPass_fst (w' : World E) (e : Edit E) (I : Iface D E) :
    ∀ (cs : List (Candidate D)) (n : Nat), (repairPass w' e I cs n).1 =
      cs.map fun c => if touches e I c then { c with status := judge I w' c } else c
  | [], _ => rfl
  | c :: cs, n => by
    unfold repairPass
    cases ht : touches e I c <;> simp [ht, repairPass_fst w' e I cs (n + 1)]

theorem repairCands_map (w' : World E) (e : Edit E) (I : Iface D E) (cs : List (Candidate D)) :
    repairCands w' e I cs =
      cs.map fun c => if touches e I c then { c with status := judge I w' c } else c :=
  repairPass_fst w' e I cs 0

/-- The pass's log is exactly the touched indices (offset `n`). -/
theorem repairPass_snd (w' : World E) (e : Edit E) (I : Iface D E) :
    ∀ (cs : List (Candidate D)) (n k : Nat), k ∈ (repairPass w' e I cs n).2 ↔
      n ≤ k ∧ ∃ c, cs[k - n]? = some c ∧ touches e I c = true
  | [], n, k => by simp [repairPass]
  | c :: cs, n, k => by
    have ih := repairPass_snd w' e I cs (n + 1) k
    unfold repairPass
    by_cases hkn : k = n
    · subst hkn
      cases ht : touches e I c
      · simp only [Bool.false_eq_true, if_false, ih]
        constructor
        · rintro ⟨h, -⟩; omega
        · rintro ⟨-, c', hc', ht'⟩
          simp at hc'; subst hc'; rw [ht] at ht'; cases ht'
      · simp [ht]
    · have hsh : ∀ (P : Prop), (n ≤ k ∧ P) ↔ (n + 1 ≤ k ∧ P) := by
        intro P; constructor <;> rintro ⟨h, hp⟩ <;> exact ⟨by omega, hp⟩
      cases ht : touches e I c
      · simp only [Bool.false_eq_true, if_false, ih]
        constructor
        · rintro ⟨h, c', hc', ht'⟩
          refine ⟨by omega, c', ?_, ht'⟩
          have : k - n = (k - (n + 1)) + 1 := by omega
          rw [this]; simpa using hc'
        · rintro ⟨h, c', hc', ht'⟩
          have h' : n + 1 ≤ k := by omega
          refine ⟨h', c', ?_, ht'⟩
          have : k - n = (k - (n + 1)) + 1 := by omega
          rw [this] at hc'; simpa using hc'
      · simp only [if_true, List.mem_cons, ih]
        constructor
        · rintro (h | ⟨h, c', hc', ht'⟩)
          · exact absurd h hkn
          · refine ⟨by omega, c', ?_, ht'⟩
            have : k - n = (k - (n + 1)) + 1 := by omega
            rw [this]; simpa using hc'
        · rintro ⟨h, c', hc', ht'⟩
          refine Or.inr ⟨by omega, c', ?_, ht'⟩
          have : k - n = (k - (n + 1)) + 1 := by omega
          rw [this] at hc'; simpa using hc'

/-- **Incremental = from scratch, at the candidate list.** On a coherent hole, re-judging only the
touched candidates gives exactly the list with EVERY candidate re-judged at the new world. -/
theorem repairCands_eq {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {e : Edit E} {cs : List (Candidate D)}
    (hs : ∀ c ∈ cs, c.status = judge I w c) :
    repairCands (e.apply w) e I cs = cs.map fun c => { c with status := judge I (e.apply w) c } := by
  rw [repairCands_map]
  apply List.map_congr_left
  intro c hc
  cases ht : touches e I c
  · simp only [Bool.false_eq_true, if_false]
    rw [judge_frame ht, ← hs c hc]
  · rfl

theorem repairHole_cands (w' : World E) (e : Edit E) (I : Iface D E) (s : Slot D) :
    (repairHole w' e I s).1.cands = repairCands w' e I s.cands := by
  unfold repairHole
  cases hT : holeTouched e I s
  · simp only [if_true]
    have hno : ∀ c ∈ s.cands, touches e I c = false := by
      intro c hc
      cases ht : touches e I c
      · rfl
      · simp only [holeTouched, Bool.or_eq_false_iff, List.any_eq_false] at hT
        exact absurd ht (hT.2 c hc)
    rw [repairCands_map]
    conv => lhs; rw [← List.map_id s.cands]
    apply List.map_congr_left
    intro c hc; simp [hno c hc]
  · simp only [Bool.true_eq_false, if_false]
    split <;> rfl

theorem repair_slot {w : World E} {e : Edit E} {cl : Closure D E} {I : Iface D E}
    (hI : I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω) :
    (repair w e cl).1.slot I.name = (repairHole (e.apply w) e I (cl.slot I.name)).1 := by
  simp [repair, find_self hI]

/-- **REPAIR CORRECTNESS.** On a coherent closure, the observable after `repair e` (every retained
candidate's status, hole by hole, in order) equals the from-scratch analysis of the template and
the same retained candidates at the new world. -/
theorem repair_agrees_from_scratch {w : World E} {e : Edit E} {cl : Closure D E}
    (hc : Coherent w cl) :
    observe (repair w e cl).1 =
      observe (fromScratch (e.apply w) cl.tpl fun u => (cl.slot u).cands) := by
  unfold observe
  apply List.map_congr_left
  intro I hI
  have hI' : I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω := hI
  rw [repair_slot (w := w) (e := e) hI', repairHole_cands, repairCands_eq (hc I hI')]
  simp [fromScratch, find_name_self cl.tpl.names hI']

/-- Repair re-establishes coherence at the new world. -/
theorem repair_coherent {w : World E} {e : Edit E} {cl : Closure D E} (hc : Coherent w cl) :
    Coherent (e.apply w) (repair w e cl).1 := by
  intro I hI c hc'
  have hI' : I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω := hI
  rw [repair_slot (w := w) (e := e) hI', repairHole_cands, repairCands_eq (hc I hI')] at hc'
  obtain ⟨c0, _, rfl⟩ := List.mem_map.1 hc'
  rfl

theorem fromScratch_coherent (w : World E) (tpl : Template D E) (cands : Nat → List (Candidate D)) :
    Coherent w (fromScratch w tpl cands) := by
  intro I hI c hc
  simp only [fromScratch, find_name_self tpl.names hI] at hc
  obtain ⟨c0, _, rfl⟩ := List.mem_map.1 hc
  rfl

/-- **LOCALITY, candidate by candidate.** Repair carries an untouched candidate unchanged (the same
record, status included): it is not re-judged. -/
theorem repair_carries_untouched {w : World E} {e : Edit E} {cl : Closure D E} {I : Iface D E}
    (hI : I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω) {i : Nat} {c : Candidate D} (hci : (cl.slot I.name).cands[i]? = some c)
    (ht : touches e I c = false) : ((repair w e cl).1.slot I.name).cands[i]? = some c := by
  rw [repair_slot hI, repairHole_cands]
  simp [repairCands_map, List.getElem?_map, hci, ht]

/-- **LOCALITY, the boundary theorem.** A token edit whose token is no dependency of any retained
candidate and backs no hole hypothesis leaves the closure exactly as it was, with no outcome and an
empty repair log (the log is emitted by the same branch of `repairPass` that re-judges). -/
theorem locality {w : World E} {t : Token} {e : Edit E} (he : e = .withdraw t ∨ e = .assert t)
    {cl : Closure D E}
    (hfree : ∀ I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω, (∀ h ∈ I.hyps, h.2 ≠ t) ∧ ∀ c ∈ (cl.slot I.name).cands, t ∉ deps I c) :
    (repair w e cl).1 = cl ∧ (repair w e cl).2.1 = [] ∧ (repair w e cl).2.2 = [] := by
  have hT : ∀ I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω, holeTouched e I (cl.slot I.name) = false := by
    intro I hI
    obtain ⟨hh, hd⟩ := hfree I hI
    simp only [holeTouched, Bool.or_eq_false_iff, List.any_eq_false]
    refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩
    · rcases he with rfl | rfl
      · simp only [hypOf, Option.isSome_map, Option.isSome_eq_false_iff, Option.isNone_iff_eq_none,
          List.find?_eq_none, decide_eq_true_eq]
        exact fun h hm => hh h hm
      · rfl
    · intro c hc
      rcases he with rfl | rfl <;> simp [touches, hd c hc]
  have hR : ∀ I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω, repairHole (e.apply w) e I (cl.slot I.name) = (cl.slot I.name, none) := by
    intro I hI; simp [repairHole, hT I hI]
  have houts : (repair w e cl).2.1 = [] := by
    simp only [repair, List.filterMap_eq_nil_iff]
    intro I hI; simp [hR I hI]
  have hlog : (repair w e cl).2.2 = [] := by
    simp only [repair, List.flatMap_eq_nil_iff, List.map_eq_nil_iff]
    intro I hI; simp [rejudgedIn, hT I hI]
  refine ⟨?_, houts, hlog⟩
  have hslot : (repair w e cl).1.slot = cl.slot := by
    funext u
    simp only [repair]
    split
    · rename_i I hf
      obtain ⟨hI, rfl⟩ := find_some hf
      rw [hR I hI]
    · rfl
  have hhist : (repair w e cl).1.hist = cl.hist := by
    have : (repair w e cl).1.hist = cl.hist ++ (repair w e cl).2.1.filterMap Outcome.event := rfl
    rw [this, houts]; simp
  cases cl
  simp only [repair] at hslot hhist ⊢
  simp only [hslot] at *
  congr

/-- Withdrawal, at the closure: after a withdrawal and repair, a `conflict` in a coherent closure
was already a `conflict`, with the same evidence, at the same index. -/
theorem withdraw_never_refutes_closure {w : World E} {t : Token} {cl : Closure D E}
    (hc : Coherent w cl) {I : Iface D E} (hI : I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω) {i : Nat} {c' : Candidate D}
    {ev : Evidence} (h : ((withdraw w t cl).1.slot I.name).cands[i]? = some c')
    (hev : c'.status = .conflict ev) :
    ∃ c, (cl.slot I.name).cands[i]? = some c ∧ c.status = .conflict ev := by
  unfold withdraw at h
  rw [repair_slot hI, repairHole_cands, repairCands_eq (hc I hI), List.getElem?_map] at h
  cases hci : (cl.slot I.name).cands[i]? with
  | none => simp [hci] at h
  | some c =>
    simp only [hci, Option.map_some, Option.some.injEq] at h
    subst h
    refine ⟨c, rfl, ?_⟩
    rw [hc I hI c (List.mem_of_getElem? hci)]
    exact withdraw_never_refutes.1 hev

/-! ## The repair log -/

theorem iface_eq_of_name {cl : Closure D E} {I J : Iface D E} (hI : I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω)
    (hJ : J ∈ cl.tpl.Ω) (hn : J.name = I.name) : J = I := by
  have h1 := find_self (cl := cl) hI
  have h2 := find_self (cl := cl) hJ
  rw [hn, h1] at h2
  exact (Option.some.inj h2).symm

/-- **LOCALITY, as work done.** The repair log names exactly the touched candidates of the closure:
`(I.name, k)` is logged iff candidate `k` of hole `I` is touched by the edit. An untouched candidate
is never re-judged (a recompute-all repair logs every candidate and fails this). -/
theorem repair_rejudged_eq_touched {w : World E} {e : Edit E} {cl : Closure D E} {I : Iface D E}
    (hI : I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω) (k : Nat) :
    (I.name, k) ∈ (repair w e cl).2.2 ↔
      ∃ c, (cl.slot I.name).cands[k]? = some c ∧ touches e I c = true := by
  simp only [repair, List.mem_flatMap, List.mem_map, Prod.mk.injEq]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨J, hJ, k', hk', hn, rfl⟩
    obtain rfl := iface_eq_of_name hI hJ hn
    unfold rejudgedIn at hk'
    split at hk'
    · cases hk'
    · simpa using (repairPass_snd _ e J _ 0 k').1 hk'
  · rintro ⟨c, hc, ht⟩
    refine ⟨I, hI, k, ?_, rfl, rfl⟩
    unfold rejudgedIn
    have hT : holeTouched e I (cl.slot I.name) = true := by
      simp only [holeTouched, Bool.or_eq_true, List.any_eq_true]
      exact Or.inr ⟨c, List.mem_of_getElem? hc, ht⟩
    simp only [hT, Bool.true_eq_false, if_false]
    exact (repairPass_snd _ e I _ 0 k).2 ⟨Nat.zero_le _, c, by simpa using hc, ht⟩


end Nanodregg.Lang.Contextual

